// NEGATIVE TEST — a chained tuple index must be parenthesised (N4-12).
//
// `p.0.1` never reaches the parser as two indices. The float rule is
// `-?[0-9]+\.[0-9]+` (`src/lexer/token.rs`), so `.0.1` after an expression is ONE
// `Float(0.1)` token, and an f64 cannot be split back into the digits that made
// it: `p.0.10` and `p.0.1` both hold 0.1, so recovering "10" would be a guess
// with a wrong answer available.
//
// The workaround is in the diagnostic and costs two characters: `(p.0).1`, which
// `tests/02_types_tuples.pd` uses and runs.
fn main() {
    let p = ((1, 2), 3);
    print_int(p.0.1);
}
